l vertices is constant? I recall that in a regular polygon, the sum of distances from any point on the circumcircle to all vertices is constant. Is that true? Let me check for a square. If P is at a vertex, the sum of distances is 0 + side + diagonal + side = 2a + d. If P is at the midpoint of an arc, the sum would be four times the distance from that midpoint to a vertex. For a square inscribed in a unit circle, the distance from a midpoint (angle \u03c0/4) to a vertex (angle 0) is 2*sin(\u03c0/8) \u2248 0.765. So sum \u2248 4*0.765 \u2248 3.06, whereas the sum when P is at a vertex is 0 + \u221a2 + 2 \u2248 3.414. Not the same. Therefore, the sum is not constant. Therefore, that approach is incorrect.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps considering complex conjugates or roots. Let me think. \n\nIf we take the sum over k even of |z - \u03c9^k| and sum over k odd of |z - \u03c9^k|. Let me consider the case where z is a root of unity. Suppose z = \u03c9^m for some m. Then PA_k = |\u03c9^m - \u03c9^k| = |\u03c9^{m - k} - 1| = 2|sin(\u03c0(m - k)/ (2n+1))|. \n\nBut for z = \u03c9^m, the sum S_even(z) = sum_{k even} |\u03c9^{m} - \u03c9^{k}| = sum_{k even} |1 - \u03c9^{k - m}|. Similarly, S_odd(z) = sum_{k odd} |1 - \u03c9^{k - m}|. But since k ranges over even or odd indices, shifting by m (which is an integer) preserves the parity. Therefore, S_even(z) = sum_{j even - m} |1 - \u03c9^j| = sum_{j even} |1 - \u03c9^j|, since shifting all indices by m (mod 2n+1) just permutes the terms. Therefore, S_even(z) is equal to the sum over even j of |1 - \u03c9^j|, which is a constant, independent of m. Similarly for S_odd(z). Therefore, for P being a vertex, the sums S_even and S_odd are constants. But earlier, in the triangle case, S_even was |1 -1| + |1 - \u03c9^2| = |1 - \u03c9^2|, and S_odd was |1 - \u03c9|. But in a triangle, |1 - \u03c9| = |1 - \u03c9^2|, since \u03c9^2 is the complex conjugate of \u03c9. Therefore, S_even = S_odd in this case. Wait, but earlier when I took P at A2 