03c9^k|. If we can show that S = 0, then the alternating sum of distances is zero, which would imply the required equality. However, absolute values make this difficult.\n\nAlternatively, consider squared distances. PA_k^2 = |z - \u03c9^k|^2 = (z - \u03c9^k)(\\overline{z} - \u03c9^{-k}) = |z|^2 + |\u03c9^k|^2 - z \u03c9^{-k} - \\overline{z} \u03c9^k. Since |z| = |\u03c9^k| =1, this simplifies to 2 - z \u03c9^{-k} - \\overline{z} \u03c9^k.\n\nTherefore, sum_{k even} PA_k^2 = sum_{k even} (2 - z \u03c9^{-k} - \\overline{z} \u03c9^k) = (n+1)*2 - z sum_{k even} \u03c9^{-k} - \\overline{z} sum_{k even} \u03c9^k.\n\nSimilarly, sum_{k odd} PA_k^2 = n*2 - z sum_{k odd} \u03c9^{-k} - \\overline{z} sum_{k odd} \u03c9^k.\n\nIf we can show that these two sums are equal, then perhaps it would help. However, the problem is about the sum of distances, not squared distances.\n\nBut maybe compute the difference between the two sums:\n\nsum_{k even} PA_k^2 - sum_{k odd} PA_k^2 = 2(n+1) - 2n - z [sum_{k even} \u03c9^{-k} - sum_{k odd} \u03c9^{-k}] - \\overline{z} [sum_{k even} \u03c9^k - sum_{k odd} \u03c9^k}].\n\nSimplify: 2 - z [sum_{k even} \u03c9^{-k} - sum_{k odd} \u03c9^{-k}] - \\overline{z} [sum_{k even} \u03c9^k - sum_{k odd} \u03c9^k}].\n\nBut sum_{k even} \u03c9^{-k} + sum_{k odd} \u03c9^{-k} = sum_{k=0}^{2n} \u03c9^{-k} = 0, since it's a geometric series of all roots of unity. Similarly for sum_{k} \u03c9^k =0. Therefore, sum_{k even} \u03c9^{-k} = - sum_{k odd} \u03c9^{-k}. Therefore, sum_{k even} \u03c9^{-k} - sum_{k odd} \u03c9^{-k} = 2 sum_{k even} \u03c9^{-k}.\n\nSimilarly for the other sum. Therefore, the expression becomes:\n\n2 - z [2 sum_{k even} \u03c9^{-k}] - \\overline{z} [2 sum_{k even} \u03c9^k}].\n\n= 2 - 2z sum_{k even} \u03c9^{-k} - 2\\overline{z} sum_{k even} \u03c9^k.\n\nBut sum_{k even} \u03c9^{-k} = sum_{k even} \u03c9^{2n+1 -k} = sum_{k even} \u03c9^{k} (since \u03c9^{2n+1} =1, and \u03c9^{-k} = \u03c9^{2n+1 -k}).\n\nWait, let's compute sum_{k even} \u