 \\left( \\frac{ \\sqrt{3}(x + \\sqrt{3} y ) }{4} \\right )^2 }\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\( \\left( \\frac{ x + \\sqrt{3} y }{4} \\right )^2 \\):\n\n\\[\n= \\sqrt{ \\left( \\frac{ x + \\sqrt{3} y }{4} \\right )^2 \\left( 1 + 3 \\right ) } = \\sqrt{ 4 \\left( \\frac{ x + \\sqrt{3} y }{4} \\right )^2 } = \\sqrt{ \\left( \\frac{ x + \\sqrt{3} y }{2} \\right )^2 } = \\frac{ | x + \\sqrt{3} y | }{2 }\n\\]\n\nBut since \\( P \\) is inside the triangle, and we're projecting onto \\( A_{3} A_{1} \\), the projection \\( P_{2} \\) should lie between \\( A_{1} \\) and \\( A_{3} \\), so \\( x + \\sqrt{3} y \\) must be positive. Therefore, we can drop the absolute value:\n\n\\[\nA_{1} P_{2} = \\frac{ x + \\sqrt{3} y }{2 }\n\\]\n\nOkay, so that's \\( A_{1} P_{2} \\). Let's try to compute \\( A_{2} P_{3} \\) and \\( A_{3} P_{1} \\) similarly.\n\nFirst, \\( A_{2} P_{3} \\): projection \\( P_{3} \\) is the projection of \\( P \\) onto side \\( A_{1} A_{2} \\). Side \\( A_{1} A_{2} \\) is from \\( (0, 0) \\) to \\( (a, 0) \\), which is the base of the triangle. The equation of this side is simply \\( y = 0 \\).\n\nThe projection of \\( P (x, y) \\) onto \\( A_{1} A_{2} \\) is straightforward: it's \\( (x, 0) \\), since projecting onto the x-axis just sets the y-coordinate to 0. Therefore, \\( P_{3} = (x, 0) \\).\n\nThen, the distance \\( A_{2} P_{3} \\) is the distance from \\( (a, 0) \\) to \\( (x, 0) \\), which is \\( |a - x| \\). Since \\( P \\) is inside the triangle, \\( x \\) must be between 0 and \\( a \\), and in an equilateral triangle, actually, the x-coordinate is constrained further because of the slope. But given that \\( P \\) is inside, \\( x \\) will be between 0 and \\( a \\), so \\( a - x \\) is positive. Therefore,\n\n\\[\nA_{2} P_{3} = a - x\n\\]\n\nNow, \\( A_{3} P_{1} \\): projection \\( P_{1} \\) is the projection of \\( P \\) onto side \\( A_{2} A_{3} \\). Side \\( A_{2} A_{3} \\) is from \\( (a, 0) \\) to \\( \\left( \\frac{a}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}a}{2} \\right ) \\). Let's find the equation of this side. The slope here is \\( \\frac{ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}a}{2} - 0 }{ \\frac{a}{2} - a } = \\frac{ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}a}{2} }{ -\\frac{a}{2} } = -\\sqrt{3} \\). So the line equation can be written as \\( y - 0 = -\\sqrt{3}(x - a) \\), simplifying to \\( y = -\\sqrt{3}x + \\sqrt{3}a \\).\n\nTo find the projection \\( P_{1} \\) of \\( P (x, y) \\) onto this line. Again, using the projection formula. Let's denote the line as \\( y = -\\sqrt{3}x + \\sqrt{3}a \\). To find the projection, we can use the formula for projecting a point onto a line.\n\nAlternatively, using vector projection. Let's consider a parametric form of the line \\( A_{2} A_{3} \\). Let's take a point \\( A_{2} (a, 0) \\) and direction vector towards \\( A_{3} \\): \\( \\vec{w} = ( -\\frac{a}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}a}{2} ) \\). So, parametric equations for the line can be written as \\( (a, 0) + t ( -\\frac{a}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}a}{2} ) \\), where \\( t \\) ranges from 0 to 1.\n\nThe vector from \\( A_{2} \\) to \\( P \\) is \\( (x - a, y - 0) = (x - a, y) \\). The projection scalar \\( t \\) onto \\( \\vec{w} \\) is:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{ (x - a)( -\\frac{a}{2} ) + y ( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}a}{2} ) }{ \\| \\vec{w} \\|^2 } \n\\]\n\nCalculating the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\| \\vec{w} \\|^2 = \\left( -\\frac{a}{2} \\right )^2 + \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}a}{2} \\right )^2 = \\frac{a^2}{4} + \\frac{3a^2}{4} = a^2\n\\]\n\nSo, numerator:\n\n\\[\n- \\frac{a}{2}(x - a) + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}a}{2} y = - \\frac{a}{2}x + \\frac{a^2}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}a}{2} y\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{ - \\frac{a}{2}x + \\frac{a^2}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}a}{2} y }{ a^2 } = \\frac{ -x + a + \\sqrt{3} y }{ 2a }\n\\]\n\nThen, the projection point \\( P_{1} \\) is:\n\n\\[\n(a, 0) + t \\cdot \\vec{w} = (a, 0) + \\frac{ -x + a + \\sqrt{3} y }{ 2a } \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}a}{2} \\right )\n\\]\n\nCalculating each coordinate:\n\nX-coordinate:\n\n\\[\na + \\frac{ -x + a + \\sqrt{3} y }{ 2a } \\