ta), -d*sin(theta))\n\nNow, we need to determine the conditions under which this quadrilateral is a trapezoid or a parallelogram.\n\nA trapezoid has at least one pair of parallel sides. Let's check the slopes of the sides.\n\nSide AB: from A(a,0) to B(b*cos(theta), b*sin(theta)).\n\nSlope of AB: (b*sin(theta) - 0)/(b*cos(theta) - a) = (b*sin(theta))/(b*cos(theta) - a)\n\nSide BC: from B to C(-c,0).\n\nSlope of BC: (0 - b*sin(theta))/(-c - b*cos(theta)) = (-b*sin(theta))/(-c - b*cos(theta)) = (b*sin(theta))/(c + b*cos(theta))\n\nSide CD: from C(-c,0) to D(-d*cos(theta), -d*sin(theta)).\n\nSlope of CD: (-d*sin(theta) - 0)/(-d*cos(theta) - (-c)) = (-d*sin(theta))/(c - d*cos(theta)) = (-d*sin(theta))/(c - d*cos(theta))\n\nSide DA: from D to A(a,0).\n\nSlope of DA: (0 - (-d*sin(theta)))/(a - (-d*cos(theta))) = (d*sin(theta))/(a + d*cos(theta))\n\nA parallelogram requires both pairs of opposite sides to be parallel. So, slope AB = slope CD, and slope BC = slope DA.\n\nLet\u2019s compute slope AB and slope CD:\n\nSlope AB: (b*sin(theta))/(b*cos(theta) - a)\n\nSlope CD: (-d*sin(theta))/(c - d*cos(theta))\n\nFor these to be equal:\n\n(b*sin(theta))/(b*cos(theta) - a) = (-d*sin(theta))/(c - d*cos(theta))\n\nAssuming sin(theta) \u2260 0 (otherwise the quadrilateral is degenerate):\n\nb/(b*cos(theta) - a) = -d/(c - d*cos(theta))\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\nb*(c - d*cos(theta)) = -d*(b*cos(theta) - a)\n\nExpand:\n\nb*c - b*d*cos(theta) = -d*b*cos(theta) + a*d\n\nSimplify:\n\nb*c - b*d*cos(theta) = -b*d*cos(theta) + a*d\n\nCancel -b*d*cos(theta) from both sides:\n\nb*c = a*d\n\nWhich is the second condition, a*d = b*c.\n\nSimilarly, for the other pair of sides, slope BC and slope DA:\n\nSlope BC: (b*sin(theta))/(c + b*cos(theta))\n\nSlope DA: (d*sin(theta))/(a + d*cos(theta))\n\nSetting them equal:\n\n(b*sin(theta))/(c + b*cos(theta)) = (d*sin(theta))/(a + d*cos(theta))\n\nAssuming sin(theta) \u2260 0:\n\nb/(c + b*cos(theta)) = d/(a + d*cos(theta))\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\nb*(a + d*