er.\n\n5. **Checking Three-Digit Numbers (100-999):**\n   - For \\( \\overline{abc} = 100a + 10b + c \\), check if \\( a! + b! + c! = 100a + 10b + c \\).\n   - **Valid:**\n     - \\( 145 \\): \\( 1! + 4! + 5! = 1 + 24 + 120 = 145 \\).\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - After exhaustive checks, the only numbers satisfying the condition are **1**, **2**, and **145**.\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe natural numbers less than 1000 that equal the sum of their digits' factorials are \\(\\boxed{1}\\), \\(\\boxed{2}\\), and \\(\\boxed{145}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe are tasked with finding natural numbers less than 1000 such that each number is equal to the sum of the factorials of its digits. We start by examining single-digit, two-digit, and three-digit numbers step-by-step.\n\n#### Step 1: Factorial Definitions\n\nFirstly, let's list the factorial of digits from 0 to 9:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n0! &= 1,\\\\\n1! &= 1,\\\\\n2! &= 2,\\\\\n3! &= 6,\\\\\n4! &= 24,\\\\\n5! &= 120,\\\\\n6! &= 720,\\\\\n7! &= 5040,\\\\\n8! &= 40320,\\\\\n9! &= 362880.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n#### Step 2: Single-Digit Numbers\n\nWe check all single-digit numbers (0 through 9) because these are themselves factorials:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 1!,\\\\\n2 &= 2!.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus, the numbers that meet the condition are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1} \\text{ and } \\boxed{2}\n\\]\n\n#### Step 3: Two-Digit Numbers\n\nFor two-digit numbers \\( ab \\) where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are digits (1 through 9 for \\( a \\) and 0 through 9 for \\( b \\)), we verify if \\( 10a + b = a! + b! \\):\n\n1. \\( a = 1 \\):\n   \n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\cdot 1 + b &= 1! + b!\\\\\n10 + b &= 1 + b!\\\\\nb! &= 9 \\quad \\text{(impossible as no single-digit factorial equals 9)}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n2. \\( a = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\cdot 2 + b &= 2! + b!\\\\\n20 + b &= 2 + b!\\\\\nb! &= 18 \\quad \\text{(impossible as no single-digit factorial equals 18)}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n3. \\( a = 3 \\)