= even). Similarly, the number of odd subsets would be O\u2096 + O\u2096. Wait, but then E_{k+1} = 2E\u2096 and O_{k+1} = 2O\u2096. But if E\u2096 = O\u2096, then E_{k+1} = O_{k+1}, so equality holds.\n\nIf k+1 is odd:\n\n- Adding k+1 to an even subset makes it odd (even + odd = odd).\n- Adding k+1 to an odd subset makes it even (odd + odd = even).\nTherefore, the number of even subsets in S_{k+1} is E\u2096 (original even) + O\u2096 (subsets formed by adding k+1 to odd subsets). Similarly, the number of odd subsets is O\u2096 (original odd) + E\u2096 (subsets formed by adding k+1 to even subsets). So E_{k+1} = E\u2096 + O\u2096, and O_{k+1} = O\u2096 + E\u2096. Hence, E_{k+1} = O_{k+1} regardless, because addition is commutative. Therefore, if for S\u2096, E\u2096 = O\u2096, then E_{k+1} = O_{k+1} = E\u2096 + O\u2096 = 2^{k} (since total subsets double each time). Wait, but 2^{k} would be total subsets of S\u2096. But in S_{k+1}, total subsets are 2^{k+1}. Hmm, but if E\u2096 = O\u2096 = 2^{k-1}, then E_{k+1} = 2^{k-1} + 2^{k-1} = 2^{k}, and similarly O_{k+1} = 2^{k}. So equality holds. Therefore, regardless of whether k+1 is odd or even, the numbers of even and odd subsets are equal. Thus, by induction, it's true for all n.\n\nWait, but hold on. The inductive step works because whether k+1 is odd or even, E_{k+1} and O_{k+1} end up equal. So this seems to hold. Therefore, part (1) is proved.\n\nAlternatively, another approach is using generating functions. The generating function for subset sums mod 2. The generating function is (1 + x)(1 + x\u00b2)(1 + x\u00b3)...(1 + x\u207f). We want the number of subsets with even sum and odd sum. If we substitute x = 1, we get 2\u207f. If we substitute x = -1, we get the difference between even and odd counts. But (1 + (-1))(1 + (-1)\u00b2)...(1 + (-1)^n). For n \u2265 1, since when n=1, (1 -1) = 0, but for n \u22652, if n is even or odd. Wait, but actually, when you plug x=-1 into the generating function, each term (1 + (-1)