gles \\( \\widehat{MEC} \\) and \\( \\widehat{MDC} \\) are supplementary because they are opposite angles formed by the intersections of line segments \\( ME \\) and \\( MD \\) with \\( AC \\) and \\( BC \\) respectively:\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{MEC} + \\widehat{MDC} = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Therefore, by the converse of the Inscribed Angle Theorem, points \\( M \\), \\( E \\), \\( C \\), and \\( D \\) lie on the same circle (i.e., they are concyclic).\n\n3. Similarly, consider the angles \\( \\widehat{MFB} \\) and \\( \\widehat{MDB} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{MFB} + \\widehat{MDB} = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   This implies that points \\( M \\), \\( F \\), \\( B \\), and \\( D \\) are also concyclic.\n\n4. Using the Inscribed Angle Theorem, we can conclude that:\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{MED} = \\widehat{MCD} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\widehat{MFD} = \\widehat{MBD}\n   \\]\n  \n5. Since \\( AB \\) and \\( AC \\) are tangent to the circle \\( \\Gamma \\) at points \\( B \\) and \\( C \\), by the Tangent-Secant Theorem, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{MCD} = \\widehat{MBF} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\widehat{MBD} = \\widehat{MCE}\n   \\]\n  \n6. By the Inscribed Angle Theorem again, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{MBF} = \\widehat{MD(F)} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\widehat{MCE} = \\widehat{MD(E)}\n   \\]\n\n7. Consequently, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{MED} = \\widehat{MDF} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\widehat{MFD} = \\widehat{MDE}\n   \\]\n\n8. Since triangles \\( EMD \\) and \\( DMF \\) share two equal angles, they are similar by AA similarity criterion.\n\n9. The similarity of these triangles gives us the ratio of their corresponding sides:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{MD}{ME} = \\frac{MF}{MD}\n   \\]\n\n10. Rearranging this ratio, we can find:\n    \\[\n    MD^2 = ME \\times MF\n    \\]\n\nThus, we have proved that:\n\\[\n\\boxed{MD^2 = ME \\times MF}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the largest four-digit n