ygon considerations and because of the interior angle $\\angle A_{1} O B_{1}=120^\\circ$, it can be deduced that quadrilateral $A_{1} KB_{1} O$ is cyclic.\n   \n   Thus, $O A_{1} = O B_{1}$ because $KO$ is a bisector of angle $\\angle K = 60^\\circ$ in the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A_1 KB_1 O\\).\n\n5. **Analyze angles involving line \\(MO\\)**:\n\n   Given that $M$ is the midpoint of $A_{1} B_{1}$, and that $O$ is equidistant from $A_{1}$ and $B_{1}$, we have:\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle M O A_{1} = 60^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n   Since $O A_{1}, O B_{1}$ are equal, the segments form an equilateral angle at $O$:\n   \n   \\[\n   \\angle A_{1} O B = \\angle B O N = 60^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conclude \\(N\\) lies on the same circle with \\(A_1\\) and \\(B_1\\)**:\n\n   Since $O N = O A_{1}$ and $O A_{1} = O B_{1}$, point $N$ also lies on the circle centered at $O$ with radius $O A_{1}$.\n\n7. **Finalize the proof of \\( \\vartriangle A_{1} B_{1} N\\)** being equilateral:\n\n   All central angles $A_{1} O B_{1}, A_{1} O N$, and $B_{1} O N$ are each $60^\\circ$. This implies that the triangle formed by these points is equilateral. Hence, $\\vartriangle A_{1} B_{1} N$ is an equilateral triangle.\n\n   Therefore, we can conclude:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\vartriangle A_1B_1N \\text{ is equilateral}}\n   \\]\n\nThis completes the proof.\n$lacksquare$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Two robots, A and B, start at points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) simultaneously and head in the same direction. When robot A reaches point \\( B \\), robot B has traveled 288 meters. When robot A catches up with robot B, robot B has traveled 336 meters. What is the distance between points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) in meters?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out this problem step by step. So we have two robots, A and B, starting from points A and B at the same time and moving in the same direction. When robot A reaches point B, ro