9 monochromatic pairs (since the minimum occurs when k=3 or 4). Three columns give 27 monochromatic pairs. There are 21 distinct pairs of rows. If you have 27 monochromatic pairs distributed over 21 row pairs, then at least one row pair must have at least two monochromatic pairs. Each monochromatic pair in a row pair is from a column. Therefore, two different columns must have the same monochromatic color (either both RR or both BB) on that row pair, forming a rectangle.\n\nTherefore, this proves that such a rectangle must exist.\n\nWait, let me verify with an example. Suppose in column 1, rows 1 and 2 are red; in column 2, rows 1 and 2 are also red. Then, the rectangle formed by columns 1-2 and rows 1-2 is all red. Similarly for blue. So if two columns have the same monochromatic color on the same pair of rows, we get a rectangle.\n\nTherefore, since there are 27 monochromatic pairs across columns and only 21 row pairs, by pigeonhole, one row pair must have two monochromatic pairs from two different columns. Since each monochromatic pair is either RR or BB, but two monochromatic pairs on the same row pair must be in the same color (otherwise, one is RR and the other is BB, which don't conflict). Wait, no. If two columns have monochromatic pairs on the same row pair, they could be RR in one column and BB in another. So in that case, it's two monochromatic pairs but different colors. So, would that form a rectangle? No, because one is RR and the other is BB. So the rectangle would have two red and two blue vertices. So that wouldn't be monochromatic.\n\nAh, so my previous reasoning is flawed. Because even if a row pair has two monochromatic pairs from different columns, if they are different colors, then we don't get a monochromatic rectangle.\n\nTherefore, I need to refine the argument. Instead of counting all monochromatic pairs, I need to count monochromatic pairs per color.\n\nSo, for each column, if it has k red points, then the number of red monochromatic pairs is C(k, 2), and blue monochromatic pairs is C(7 - k, 2). The total monochromatic pairs per column is C(k, 2) + C(7 - k, 2). The minimum total is 9 when k=3 or 4, as before. However, the split between red and blue can vary.\n\nTherefore, the total number of red monochromatic pairs across all columns is the sum over columns of C(k_i, 2), where k_i is the number of red points in column i. Similarly, the total number of blue monochromatic pairs is the sum over columns of C(7 - k_i, 2). The total monochromatic pairs (red and blue) is 27 as before.\n\nBut if I want to ensure that there is a pair of rows with two monochromatic pairs of the same color, I need to consider that the 27 monochromatic pairs are split between red and blue. The worst case is when they are split as evenly as possible. For example, if 14 are red and 13 are blue. Then, distributing 14 red monochromatic pairs over 21 row pairs, by pigeonhole, at least ceiling(14 / 21) = 1 per pair, which doesn't help. Similarly for blue. Wait, this approach may not work.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we need to use double counting or another combinatorial method.\n\nWait, here's a different approach inspired by the standard grid Ramsey problems. Consider each column as a 7-bit binary string. We need to show that in any three such strings, there exists a pair of coordinates (rows) where two columns have the same bit (color) in both coordinates.\n\nThis is similar to the concept of \"correlated\" pairs between columns. If we can show that two columns must agree on at least one pair of rows for both bits, then we have our rectangle.\n\nThe number of pairs of rows is 21. For two columns, the number of pairs of rows where they have the same color in both rows is equal to the number of monochromatic pairs they share. How can we compute this?\n\nAlternatively, think of each column as a vector in a 7-dimensional space over GF(2). The problem reduces to finding two vectors (columns) that have two coordinates (rows) where they both are 0 or both are 1. But I don't think linear algebra helps here directly.\n\nWait, let me recall a theorem: the Erdos-Szekeres theorem, or maybe the theorem about grids and Ramsey numbers. Wait, actually, I remember a classic problem: in a 3x7 grid, any two-coloring has a monochromatic rectangle. The proof usually uses the pigeonhole principle on the columns and rows.\n\nLet me try to reconstruct that proof.\n\nConsider each column (there are 3 columns) and the coloring of the 7 rows in that column. For each column, how many possible colorings are there of the 7 rows? 2^7=128. But we are looking for pairs of rows in the same column that are monochromatic. Wait, maybe another way.\n\nFor each column, consider the colors of the 7 rows as a 7-length sequence. Now, in each column, there are C(7,2)=21 pairs of rows. Each pair is either monochromatic (both red or both blue) or mixed. To avoid a monochromatic rectangle, we need that for every pair of columns, there is no pair of rows that is monochromatic in both columns.\n\nSo, suppose we want to avoid such a rectangle. Then, for every pair of columns, and every pair of rows, the pair of rows cannot be monochromatic in both columns. That is, if two columns share a monochromatic pair on the same rows, but with different colors, is that allowed? Wait, no. If column 1 has rows 1 and 2 red, and column 2 has rows 1 and 2 blue, then the rectangle formed by columns 1-2 and rows 1-2 has two red and two blue vertices, which is not monochromatic. So that is allowed. But if both columns have rows 1 and 2 red, then we have a red rectangle.\n\nTherefore, to avoid a monochromatic rectangle, for every pair of columns, every pair of rows must not be monochromatic in both columns with the same color.\n\nBut perhaps even more strict: even if they are different colors, but same in both columns, but that's not possible. If in column 1, pair (1,2) is red, and in column 2, pair (1,2) is blue, then the rectangle is not monochromatic. So the problem is only when two columns have the same pair of rows monochromatic in the same color.\n\nTherefore, to avoid a monochromatic rectangle, for any two columns, there must be no pair of rows that is monochromatic in both columns with the same color.\n\nNow, let's consider how many pairs of rows there are: 21. Each pair of columns (there are C(3,2)=3 pairs of columns) must avoid sharing any monochromatic pair with the same color.\n\nFor each pair of columns, how many pairs of rows can they share a monochromatic pair? For each pair of columns, if they don't share any monochromatic pair, then we're okay. But if they share even one monochromatic pair with the same color, we get a rectangle.\n\nTherefore, to avoid a monochromatic rectangle, for each pair of columns, they must not share any monochromatic pair of the same color.\n\nBut how does this affect the coloring?\n\nLet me think of it per column. For each column, the number of monochromatic pairs (red or blue) is C(k,2) + C(7 - k, 2), where k is the number of reds. As before, the minimum number is 9. So each column must have at least 9 monochromatic pairs.\n\nTherefore, three columns have at least 27 monochromatic pairs. Now, these monochromatic pairs are spread over 21 row pairs. So even if each row pair had at most one monochromatic pair from each color, but since there are two colors, maybe?\n\nWait, but each monochromatic pair is either red or blue. So for a given row pair, if two different columns have a red monochromatic pair on it, then that's two red monochromatic pairs, leading to a red rectangle. Similarly for blue.\n\nAlternatively, if a row pair has one red monochromatic pair in one column and one blue monochromatic pair in another column, that's allowed. But if a row pair has two red or two blue monochromatic pairs from different columns, then that's a rectangle.\n\nTherefore, to avoid a rectangle, each row pair can have at most one red and one blue monochromatic pair across all columns. Therefore, f