olumn's color must differ, but the way the pairs are structured leads to overlapping in the same color.\n\nBut actually, in the example above, column 3's red pairs overlap with column 1's red pairs, leading to a rectangle. Hence, the conclusion is that such a rectangle must exist.\n\nTherefore, the proof is that with three columns, each having at least nine monochromatic pairs, and only 21 row pairs, two columns must share a monochromatic pair on the same rows, leading to a rectangle.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\text{Such a rectangle always exists.}}", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that any 2-coloring of the $3 \\times 7$ grid $D = \\{(x, y) \\mid 1 \\leqslant x \\leq 3, 1 \\leqslant y \\leq 7\\}$ will always contain a monochromatic rectangle with sides parallel to the coordinate axes, we can use the following reasoning:\n\n1. **Monochromatic Pairs in Columns**:\n   - Each column in the grid has 7 rows. For each column, the number of monochromatic pairs (either red-red or blue-blue) of rows is given by $C(k, 2) + C(7 - k, 2)$, where $k$ is the number of red rows in the column. The minimum number of monochromatic pairs occurs when $k = 3$ or $k = 4$, resulting in $C(3, 2) + C(4, 2) = 3 + 6 = 9$ monochromatic pairs per column.\n\n2. **Total Monochromatic Pairs**:\n   - With 3 columns, the total number of monochromatic pairs across all columns is at least $3 \\times 9 = 27$.\n\n3. **Pigeonhole Principle**:\n   - There are $C(7, 2) = 21$ pairs of rows in the grid. By the pigeonhole principle, distributing 27 monochromatic pairs over 21 row pairs implies that at least one pair of rows must be monochromatic in two different columns.\n\n4. **Forming a Rectangle**:\n   - If two columns share a monochromatic pair (either red-red or blue-blue) on the same pair of rows, this forms a rectangle with vertices of the same color. This is because the same pair of rows in two different columns will have the same color at all four vertices, forming a rectangle.\n\n5. **Conclusion*