rac{\\pi}{2} - \\alpha\\right) = \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^2 \\alpha + \\cos^2 \\alpha = 1\n   \\]\n   Which is true, confirming the solution.\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\alpha + \\beta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Given the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\beta = \\sin(\\alpha + \\beta)\n   \\]\n   we need to prove that:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha + \\beta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n2. Applying the Pythagorean identity and the sum of angles formula, start by rewriting \\(\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sin(\\alpha + \\beta) = \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\beta.\n   \\]\n\n3. Substitute this into the original equation:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\beta = \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\beta.\n   \\]\n\n4. Rearrange the equation to group all terms on one side:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\beta = 0.\n   \\]\n\n5. Next, to facilitate analysis, rewrite the equation as:\n   \\[\n   (\\sin \\alpha - \\cos \\beta) \\sin \\alpha = (\\sin \\beta - \\cos \\alpha) \\sin \\beta.\n   \\]\n\n6. Consider distinct cases to check for contradictions.\n\n   **Case 1: \\( \\sin \\alpha > \\cos \\beta \\) and \\( \\cos \\alpha > \\sin \\beta \\)**\n   - From \\( 1 = \\sin^2 \\alpha + \\cos^2 \\alpha \\) and \\( 1 = \\sin^2 \\beta + \\cos^2 \\beta \\), combining these:\n   - Suppose \\( \\sin^2 \\alpha = x \\) and \\( \\cos^2 \\alpha = 1 - x \\).\n     \\[\n     x + (1 - x) = 1 \\quad \\text{(Valid as per identity)}\n     \\]\n   - Assume \\( \\sin^2 \\beta = y \\) and \\( \\cos^2 \\beta = 1 - y \\).\n     \\[\n     y + (1 - y) = 1 \\quad \\text{(Valid as per identity)}\n     \\]\n   - Suppose \\( \\sin \\alpha > \\cos \\beta \\) and \\( \\cos \\alpha > \\sin \\beta \\), it would imply:\n     \\[\n     1 > (\\sin^2 \\beta + \\cos^2 \\beta) = 1 \\quad \\