tely 206.57 degrees.\n\nSo \u03b1 \u2248 3.605 radians, then \u03b2 = 2\u03c0 - \u03b1 \u2248 2\u03c0 - 3.605 \u2248 2.678 radians (about 153.43 degrees).\n\nLet's compute sin \u03b1 + 2 cos \u03b2 at this \u03b1 and \u03b2.\n\nFirst, compute sin(3.605):\n\n3.605 radians is 3\u03c0/2 - arctan(2). Let's compute sin(3\u03c0/2 - arctan(2)) = sin(3\u03c0/2)cos(arctan(2)) - cos(3\u03c0/2)sin(arctan(2)) = (-1)(1/\u221a5) - 0*(2/\u221a5) = -1/\u221a5 \u2248 -0.447.\n\nThen 2 cos \u03b2 = 2 cos(2\u03c0 - \u03b1) = 2 cos \u03b1. Compute cos(3.605):\n\ncos(3.605) = cos(3\u03c0/2 - arctan(2)) = cos(3\u03c0/2)cos(arctan(2)) + sin(3\u03c0/2)sin(arctan(2)) = 0*(1/\u221a5) + (-1)*(2/\u221a5) = -2/\u221a5 \u2248 -0.894.\n\nTherefore, sin \u03b1 + 2 cos \u03b2 \u2248 -1/\u221a5 + 2*(-2/\u221a5) = -1/\u221a5 - 4/\u221a5 = -5/\u221a5 = -\u221a5 \u2248 -2.236. So indeed, this is the minimum on the boundary \u03b1 + \u03b2 = 2\u03c0.\n\nTherefore, this suggests that the minimum value of the expression is -\u221a5 \u2248 -2.236, achieved when \u03b1 = 3\u03c0/2 - arctan(2) and \u03b2 = \u03c0/2 + arctan(2). Wait, let me check \u03b2:\n\nSince \u03b2 = 2\u03c0 - \u03b1, and \u03b1 = 3\u03c0/2 - arctan(2), so \u03b2 = 2\u03c0 - (3\u03c0/2 - arctan(2)) = \u03c0/2 + arctan(2). Which is approximately \u03c0/2 + 1.107 \u2248 2.678 radians, as before.\n\nTherefore, this critical point on the boundary \u03b1 + \u03b2 = 2\u03c0 gives a lower value than the previous points we considered (like \u03b1 = \u03c0, \u03b2 = \u03c0). So my initial reasoning was incomplete because I didn't consider this critical point on the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the minimum value is actually -\u221a5. To confirm, let's check if this point satisfies all constraints. \u03b1 \u2248 3.605 radians (which is less than 2\u03c0), \u03b2 \u2248 2.678 radians (also less than 2\u03c0), and \u03b1 + \u03b2 = 2\u03c0, which satisfies the constraint.\n\nTherefore, the minimum occurs on the boundary \u03b1 + \u03b2 = 2\u03c0, and the