f \\( \\sin \\theta + 2 \\sin \\varphi \\),\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\theta + 2 \\sin \\varphi.\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\( \\theta \\) and \\(\\varphi\\) are between \\(0\\) and \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), the functions \\( \\sin \\theta \\) and \\( \\sin \\varphi \\) are both increasing. The sum \\( \\theta + \\varphi = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\) achieves the maximum value of the expression \\( \\sin \\theta + 2 \\sin \\varphi \\). Hence,\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\theta + 2 \\sin \\varphi = \\cos \\varphi + 2 \\sin \\varphi.\n\\]\n\nLet us functionally maximize \\(\\cos \\varphi + 2 \\sin \\varphi\\):\n\nUsing the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we know:\n\n\\[ \nx \\cos \\varphi + y \\sin \\varphi \\leq \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\varphi + 2 \\sin \\varphi \\leq \\sqrt{1 + 4} = \\sqrt{5}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the original problem statement\n\n\\[\n-\\left(\\sin \\theta + 2 \\sin \\varphi \\right) \\geq -\\sqrt{5}.\n\\]\n\nSo, the minimum value of \\( \\sin \\alpha + 2 \\cos \\beta \\) is\n\n\\(\\boxed{-\\sqrt{5}}\\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A frustum of a cone with a height of 8 contains a sphere \\( O_1 \\) with a radius of 2. The center of sphere \\( O_1 \\) lies on the axis of the frustum. Sphere \\( O_1 \\) is tangent to the upper base and the side surface of the frustum. Another sphere \\( O_2 \\) with a radius of 3 can be placed inside the frustum such that it is tangent to sphere \\( O_1 \\), the lower base of the frustum, and the side surface of the frustum at one point each. Excluding sphere \\( O_2 \\), what is the maximum number of additional spheres with a radius of 3 that can be placed inside the frustum?\n(A) 1\n(B) 2\n(C) 3\n(D) 4", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to tackle this geometry problem step by step. Hmm, it's about a frustum of a cone containing two spheres, O\u2081 and O\u2082. The question asks for the maximum number of additional spheres of radius 3 that can fit inside the fr