upper base), we find \\( R \\approx 17.35 \\) and \\( r \\approx 0.45 \\).  \n\n4. **Spheres at Height 3**:\n   - Spheres of radius 3 must be tangent to the lower base (centers at height 3) and the side surface.  \n   - The radius of the frustum at height 3 is \\( \\approx 11.01 \\). Centers of spheres are 3 units inward from the side surface, forming a circle of radius \\( \\approx 8.01 \\).  \n\n5. **Arrangement of Spheres**:\n   - The circumference of the circle at height 3 is \\( 2\\pi \\times 8.01 \\approx 50.34 \\).  \n   - Each sphere requires 6 units of arc length (diameter 3).  \n   - Number of spheres: \\( \\approx 50.34 / 6 \\approx 8.39 \\), but due to 3D packing constraints, the effective number is determined by central angles.  \n\n6. **Central Angle Calculation**:\n   - Chord length between centers = 6 units.  \n   - For a circle of radius 8.01, the central angle per sphere is \\( \\theta = 2\\arcsin(3/8.01) \\approx 44^\\circ \\).  \n   - Total spheres: \\( 360^\\circ / 44^\\circ \\approx 8 \\), but this overestimates due to the frustum's slope.  \n\n7. **Realistic Packing**:\n   - Considering the slope and cross-section analysis, the maximum number of spheres arranged symmetrically around the axis is 3 (including \\( O_2 \\)).  \n   - Excluding \\( O_2 \\), the maximum additional spheres are **2**.\n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\boxed{B}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understanding the problem setup**:\n   - We have a cylindrical frustum with a height of 8 units.\n   - There is a sphere \\(O_1\\) inside the frustum with a radius of 2 units. \n   - The center of the sphere \\(O_1\\) is located on the axis of the frustum.\n   - Sphere \\(O_1\\) touches the side and bottom surface of the frustum.\n\n2. **Position of sphere \\(O_2\\) with radius 3 units**:\n   - Sphere \\(O_2\\) also needs to be placed inside the frustum such that it touches sphere \\(O_1\\), the side surface, and the bottom surface of the frustum at exactly one point each.\n   \n3. **Es